# 1

> [!Title]
> 'World war, above anything else, explains why India had become independent by 1947.' Assess the validity of this view.

1. World War
2. Nationalism
3. Metropole factors outside of World War

India would find independence shortly after the Second World War in 1947, this was the end of the 90 year Raj period. This seemingly sudden shift and decolonisation can be largely attributed to changing circumstances for the British. These changes include a shifting domestic priority leading to a further disdain for empire and its costly nature. This change and its economic demands were only exacerbated by the World War. In this way it was not solely the world war that led to independence but it did heavily push towards it and when combined with other metropole based factors it led to independence. While undoubtedly also a factor the nationalist movement of India was not nearly as significant as the other factors noted. Overall, it was a combination of metropole factors worsened by the World War that would lead to independence.

# 2

> [!Title]
> 'After 1945, Britain decolonised primarily because of changed international circumstances.' Assess the validity of this view.

1. International circumstances
2. Nationalism
3. Metropole factors

After 1945 Britain entered a period of rapid decolonisation. This would lead to the 1967 Suez crisis where the British would withdraw all troops east of Suez effectively ending the empire. International circumstances had undoubtedly changed with the two new great powers being starkly anti-colonial. While this factor had some influence in some colonies it is far from the primary reason decolonisation occurred. Nationalist movements grew in strength and effectiveness during this period and would lead to decolonisation in some colonies and areas. However, this was still far from the most influential factor in most cases. The most influential factor by far during this period was the many changes that occurred in the metropole. These changes include many political and economic shifts that pushed the empire out of the public's and government's priorities. There was also vast cultural changes with an end to deference. These factors led to a reluctance towards empire that would lead to decolonisation. Overall, this factor undoubtedly was the most influential when considering the reasons for decolonisation in the period following WW2.

# 3

> [!Title]
> 'British expansion in Africa was driven by a desire to spread "Christianity and commerce."' Assess the validity of this view in relation to the period 1857-1900.

1. Christianity
2. Commerce
3. Political/strategical

The British would begin to colonise further in Africa during the latter parts of the 19th century, this is particularly notable during the scramble for Africa in the 1880s onwards. While some expansion can be attributed to the 'Flag following Faith' this is rare and hardly notable. There is limited cases of this and other factors drove more. Commerce is more convincing as many British colonies brought a vast amount of wealth to the empire. Many chartered companies were used to consolidate economic power. However, many colonies were either unprofitable or were taken for other reasons and just happened to be profitable. The factor that drove expansion the most was instead strategic and tactical interests. Much expansion in East Africa was done in an effort to protect the Suez Canal and the route to India it provided. Much expansion was also done to block or compete with other powers most notably France. The most colonies can be explained by this factor and therefore it was the largest driving force behind African colonisation during this period.

